  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday night through Monday)\nIssued at 240 AM CDT Tue Oct 13 2026\n\nBy Wednesday evening, the large upper low moving across the Rockies \nand approaches just to the north of our area. Guidance continues to \nshow ample moisture areawide and with improving upper level support, \nwill lead to scattered to numerous showers and storms. Wednesday \nnight through Thursday and into early Friday will see medium to high \n(40-80%) chances stretching from Big Bend northward into the Permian \nBasin. Multiple rounds of showers and storms will occur during that \nWednesday to early Friday timeframe. Given the tropical origin of \nthe airmass, any convection will be efficient at producing rain and \nbring about a threat for flash flooding each day. Rain amounts will \nvary greatly as it will be dependent on where storms develop during \neach round. At any rate, urban, low lying areas, and the rougher \nterrain portions of West Texas will be at significant risk for flash \nflooding even when it may not be actively raining in that particular \nlocation. There will be breaks in the rain between rounds of \nprecipitation each day, but not long enough breaks for rain to soak \nin at all locations. \n\nA cold front is pushed through the region on Thursday and keeps \ntemperatures in the 70s for most and brings lows into the 50s that \nevening. Continuing cloud cover and another round of rain on Friday \nwill keep highs near 60F. Strong post frontal winds will be seen\nacross the higher elevations late Thursday and into Friday. Into \nthe weekend, below normal temperatures hold on, but a gradual \nwarmup is expected by early next week. Long range guidance agrees \non a slight drying out this coming weekend, but keep low (10-30%) \nrain chances over the western portions of the CWA. \n\n-Stickney",
